    <body>
      <sec id="article-107450.s1" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>The Meckel diverticulum is a common congenital gastrointestinal malformation of the ileum, resulting from incomplete atrophy of the vitelline duct during embryogenesis. Incomplete regression of the omphalomesenteric duct forms a blind pouch at the antimesenteric border of the gut.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r1">[1]</xref> Most cases follow the &#x0201c;rule of 2,&#x0201d; indicating that the Meckel diverticulum occurs in 2% of the population, typically presents before 2 years of age, is twice as common in male as in female individuals, measures approximately 2 inches in length, lies about 2 feet from the ileocecal junction, and may contain heterotopic mucosa. This pattern is not universal across all presentations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>A symptomatic Meckel diverticulum is often associated with ectopic tissue. Ileal mucosa is present along with gastric or pancreatic tissue in the walls of the diverticulum. Less commonly, ectopic duodenal or colonic tissue may be observed. The most frequent causes of symptomatic Meckel diverticulum include intestinal obstruction, gastrointestinal hemorrhage, and inflammation of the diverticulum, with or without perforation. Rare manifestations include umbilical abnormalities related to the vitelline duct and Meckelian neoplasms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Diagnosis of Meckel diverticulum&#x02013;related disorders is often challenging, with imaging playing a critical role in prompt recognition and differentiation from other conditions with similar clinical presentations. The most accurate imaging modality for identifying Meckel diverticula containing ectopic gastric mucosa is the technetium-99m (99mTc) pertechnetate Meckel scan, a scintigraphic study with reported accuracy of 90% in the pediatric population.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;The radiotracer 99mTc pertechnetate is absorbed by mucin-producing cells of the gastric mucosa and secreted into the intestinal lumen.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r5">[5]</xref> Accumulation in the gastric mucosa renders scintigraphy the study of choice for detecting ectopic tissue. When performed correctly, Meckel diverticulum scintigraphy achieves approximately 100% sensitivity and specificity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Indications for Meckel scintigraphy include the localization of ectopic gastric mucosa in cases of unexplained gastrointestinal bleeding. The procedure is optimal in patients not actively bleeding. In cases of active hemorrhage, a 99mTc&#x02013;labeled red blood cell (RBC) scan is the preferred imaging modality.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-107450.s2" sec-type="Specimen Collection">
        <title>Specimen Collection</title>
        <p>Traditionally, specimen collection occurred through open diverticulectomy. Recent advancements in laparoscopic techniques have enabled management via minimally invasive laparoscopic procedures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r8">[8]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-107450.s3" sec-type="Procedures">
        <title>Procedures</title>
        <p>Scintigraphy with&#x000a0;99mTc&#x02013;pertechnetate is the modality of choice for evaluating patients with a suspected Meckel diverticulum,&#x000a0;widely referred to as "Meckel scan." When performed correctly, this technique detects Meckel diverticula with approximately 100% sensitivity and specificity. Various imaging modalities remain under consideration for diagnosing Meckel diverticulum, particularly in adults, where diagnostic accuracy is often insufficient for routine clinical practice.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>Mucoid cells of the gastric mucosa within the Meckel diverticulum secrete chloride into the intestinal lumen, independent of parietal cell presence. The&#x000a0;radiopharmaceutical&#x000a0;99mTc&#x02013;pertechnetate behaves as a halide anion, such as chloride or iodide. Both normal and ectopic mucoid cells actively accumulate the radiotracer from the bloodstream and secrete it into the intestinal lumen.</p>
        <p>According to guidelines from the Society of Nuclear Medicine and Molecular Imaging, fasting is not required for the examination. However, preexamination fasting of 3 to 4 hours may improve sensitivity for detecting ectopic gastric mucosa.</p>
        <p>All drugs or procedures that may irritate the gastrointestinal tract should be discontinued 2 to 3 days prior to the study when feasible. Premedication with a histamine&#x000a0;receptor type 2 (H2R) antagonist, a proton pump inhibitor, or glucagon can enhance study sensitivity. Administration of an H2R blocker or proton pump inhibitor occurs before injection of the radiopharmaceutical agent.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;Multiple studies have demonstrated that pantoprazole provides scan quality and lesion-to-background activity ratios comparable to those of ranitidine for detecting Meckel diverticula. Pantoprazole represents a reliable alternative in clinical protocols when ranitidine is unavailable.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>An intravenous dose of 296 to 444 MBq (8&#x02013;12 mCi) is administered in adults. The recommended pediatric dose is 1.85 MBq/kg (0.05 mCi/kg).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r12">[12]</xref> The European Association of Nuclear Medicine (EANM) provides a dedicated pediatric dosage calculator to ensure accurate weight-based radiopharmaceutical dosing in accordance with established guidelines.</p>
        <p>Imaging is performed with the patient supine, including the abdomen and pelvis within the field of view. For infants and small children up to age 2 years, the thorax should also be included to evaluate for bronchopulmonary foregut malformations containing ectopic mucosa. Dynamic flow images up to 1 minute are acquired to identify vascular blood pool activity that may be mistaken for ectopic gastric mucosa.</p>
        <p>Anterior abdominal images are obtained for at least 30 minutes. Imaging beyond 60 minutes may reduce sensitivity and complicate interpretation due to the passage of activity from the stomach to the small intestine. Additional static images, particularly lateral views, improve the localization of renal pelvic activity. Postvoiding images may aid in detecting Meckel diverticulum activity obscured by the bladder.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-107450.s4" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>The Meckel scan is indicated for the identification of a Meckel diverticulum, though it may also detect&#x000a0;ectopic gastric mucosa&#x000a0;in conditions like Barrett esophagus with dysplasia. This modality cannot identify Barrett esophagus without dysplasia, as the absence of mucoid cells prevents the secretion of 99mTc&#x02013;pertechnetate.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>The study is contraindicated in patients who have recently undergone an <italic toggle="yes">in vivo</italic>&#x000a0;RBC labeling scan using a pyrophosphate kit, in which circulating RBCs are treated with stannous ion via intravenous administration. Such labeling may interfere with Meckel scan accuracy because intravenous 99mTc&#x02013;pertechnetate will bind to RBCs rather than accumulate in ectopic gastric mucosa. This limitation does not apply when RBC labeling is performed using the <italic toggle="yes">in vitro</italic> method.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r14">[14]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-107450.s5" sec-type="Potential Diagnosis">
        <title>Potential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Differentials for Meckel scintigraphy include normal variants (eg, bowel transit, urinary activity) and rare ectopic gastric sites. Both Meckel diverticula and Barrett esophagus with dysplasia may show localized mucosal uptake due to mucoid cell secretion of 99mTc-pertechnetate, potentially mimicking other gastrointestinal lesions with ectopic mucosa.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-107450.s6" sec-type="Normal and Critical Findings">
        <title>Normal and Critical Findings</title>
        <p>Patients with Meckel diverticula and Barrett esophagus demonstrate increased secretion of 99mTc&#x02013;pertechnetate.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;The flow phase becomes visible during tracer administration. Typical structures observed in this phase include the heart, lungs, major arteries and veins, and vascular organs such as the spleen, liver, and kidneys. Maximum accumulation of radioactivity occurs in the stomach during the dynamic phase, approximately 10 to 15 minutes after injection. Activity in the kidneys, ureters, and bladder is considered normal. Duodenal, jejunal, and occasionally ileal activity may also be visualized and should not be mistaken for ectopic mucosa.</p>
        <p>Meckel diverticula are most commonly located in the right lower quadrant but may occur elsewhere (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Meckel Diverticulum Scintigraphy). Lateral views obtained at the end of the study are essential to differentiate anterior structures, such as Meckel diverticula, from posterior structures, including the kidneys, ureters, and bladder. Hyperactivity within Meckel diverticula is typically prominent, rounded, small in size, and appears simultaneously with stomach activity between 10 and 20 minutes after tracer injection. Radioactivity within the diverticulum increases in parallel with gastric activity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>Evaluation of Barrett esophagus requires continuous suction of radioactive saliva. Cephalad migration of gastric activity into the esophagus indicates the presence of ectopic gastric mucosa, but hiatal hernia and gastroesophageal reflux must be excluded before interpretation.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-107450.s7" sec-type="Interfering Factors">
        <title>Interfering Factors</title>
        <p>Patients with a negative Meckel scan but high clinical suspicion of Meckel diverticulum may undergo a repeat scan to confirm the diagnosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;False-positive results can arise from ectopic gastric mucosa within an enteric duplication cyst or from other bowel diseases, such as intussusception and small bowel obstruction. Bowel cleansing using enemas or laxatives may induce inflammation that accumulates the tracer. Peptic ulcers and vascular lesions with increased blood pool, including arteriovenous malformations and hemangiomas, can retain tracer longer than healthy tissue, producing a false-positive appearance. Focal tracer accumulation in the genitourinary tract, such as uterine blush, hydronephrosis, ectopic kidney, extrarenal pelvis, hydroureter, vesicourethral reflux, and bladder diverticulum, may also generate false-positive findings.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r19">[19]</xref></p>
        <p>False-negative results are typically caused by gastrointestinal bleeding during tracer circulation, tracer activity obscured by the urinary bladder, or normal activity in dilated ureters. Postvoiding images are essential to distinguish Meckel diverticulum activity from normal urinary excretion. Small foci of ectopic mucosa, smaller than 1.8 cm2, or motion artifacts can produce false-negative findings. Prior barium fluoroscopy, perchlorate administration, or the presence of ectopic tissue other than gastric mucosa may also result in false-negative outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>Guidelines and contemporary reviews continue to support H2R antagonists&#x02014;classically cimetidine and, historically, ranitidine or famotidine&#x02014;to reduce tracer washout from ectopic gastric mucosa and enhance lesion conspicuity. Practice guidelines from the&#x000a0;Society of Nuclear Medicine and Molecular Imaging and the&#x000a0;European Association of Nuclear Medicine&#x000a0;include these agents as part of scan optimization, and mechanistic and clinical studies demonstrate improved detection with cimetidine pretreatment.</p>
        <p>Many centers incorporate&#x000a0;single-photon emission computed tomography (SPECT) or&#x000a0;SPECT combined with computed tomography (SPECT/CT) following planar imaging to improve anatomic localization, such as distinguishing true ectopic gastric uptake from urinary activity, and increase diagnostic confidence, particularly when planar findings are equivocal (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Meckel Diverticulum Scintigraphy and Single Photon Emission Computed Tomography). A Meckel diverticulum does not always contain ectopic gastric mucosa. When heterotopic tissue is present, it may include pancreatic, colonic, or other intestinal-type epithelium, and some lesions consist solely of normal ileal mucosa.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-107450.s8" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Adverse effects associated with radiopharmaceutical administration are generally uncommon but can include chills, nausea, vomiting, diffuse rash, pruritus, hives or urticaria, chest pain or discomfort, hypertension, dizziness or vertigo, headache, diaphoresis, and, rarely, anaphylaxis.&#x000a0;Additional complications may result from misadministration, vasovagal reactions, or injury due to poor injection technique.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r21">[21]</xref> The liposomal formulation of the radiopharmaceutical reportedly exhibits a lower incidence of adverse effects.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r22">[22]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-107450.s9" sec-type="Patient Safety and Education">
        <title>Patient Safety and Education</title>
        <p>Patient education regarding potential side effects of 99mTc&#x02013;pertechnetate and the possibility of repeat imaging in cases of negative results is essential, particularly when clinical suspicion of Meckel diverticulum is high. Epinephrine may be administered subcutaneously, intramuscularly, or intravenously in severe anaphylactic reactions. Aminophylline is an alternative when antihistamine therapy provides an inadequate response. The International Commission on Radiological Protection recommends interruption of breastfeeding for 12 hours following administration of 99mTc&#x02013;pertechnetate.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r23">[23]</xref> A pregnancy test is advised prior to administration of any radiopharmaceutical that could expose the fetus to more than 50 mGy.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-107450.s10" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>Meckel diverticula can present with signs and symptoms of small bowel obstruction. These outpouchings&#x000a0;may also manifest&#x000a0;with rectal bleeding, which may be painless&#x000a0;or&#x000a0;occur with peritonitis. Common presentations include abdominal pain, vomiting, fever, and bloody stools. Multiple imaging modalities, including abdominal ultrasound, abdominal radiography, abdominal angiography, abdominopelvic computed tomography, and magnetic resonance imaging, demonstrate lower sensitivity and specificity for Meckel diverticula compared with 99mTc&#x02013;pertechnetate scintigraphy (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Perforated Meckel Diverticulum on Computed Tomography).</p>
        <p>Scintigraphy with 99mTc&#x02013;pertechnetate detects Meckel diverticula by exploiting the physiological secretion of the radiopharmaceutical by the gastric mucosa, including ectopic gastric tissue within the diverticulum. When performed correctly, this method achieves approximately 100% sensitivity and specificity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r24">[24]</xref> Diagnosis of Meckel diverticulum remains challenging, and the condition should be considered even after an initially&#x000a0;normal&#x000a0;result, as some patients exhibit atypical presentations that require surgical intervention for confirmation.</p>
        <p>A symptomatic Meckel diverticulum is treated by resection, performed either via laparoscopy or open surgery. Management of an incidentally discovered Meckel diverticulum remains debated, although current evidence generally supports resection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-107450.r25">[25]</xref> Complications following resection can occur, with wound infection representing the most common, followed by postoperative ileus.</p>
      </sec>
