    <back>
      <floats-group id="article-107450.s12">
        <fig id="article-107450.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Meckel Diverticulum Scintigraphy. Sequential anterior abdominal images demonstrate a focal area of increased radiotracer uptake in the right lower quadrant. This activity appears simultaneously with the gastric mucosa and persists throughout the 24-minute study. Findings are consistent with a Meckel diverticulum containing ectopic gastric tissue. Contributed by S Dulebohn, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Meckels__scan__nucs" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-107450.s13">
        <fig id="article-107450.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Perforated Meckel Diverticulum on Computed Tomography.&#x000a0;Coronal, sagittal, and axial views reveal a blind-ending, fluid-filled pouch arising from the distal ileum. Extraluminal air and surrounding inflammatory changes indicate perforation of the Meckel diverticulum. Associated fat stranding and localized fluid collection are visible in the lower abdomen. Contributed by Peter Graves, MD, FACEP</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Comb18042018061456" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-107450.s14">
        <fig id="article-107450.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Meckel Diverticulum Scintigraphy and Single Photon Emission Computed Tomography. Planar 30-minute imaging and Single Photon Emission Computed Tomography (SPECT) maximum intensity projection show focal radiotracer uptake, though precise anatomical localization is limited. Combined SPECT and computed tomography (CT) fusion images provide superior spatial resolution, definitively localizing the periumbilical ectopic gastric mucosa within a Meckel diverticulum. This advanced imaging modality differentiates true diverticular activity from atypical inflammatory bowel uptake, facilitating precise surgical planning. Contributed by Mehdi Djekidel MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Meckels__Scan" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
